00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.010 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Baker Lake between of Canada's better  known Arctic communities it's famous for  both its artists and its industry but on  any given day life here looks a lot like  anywhere else in small-town Canada  community events are the main attraction  pretty much everyone is crazy about  hockey and family is everything  [Music]  but along with the good comes the  challenges the same ones that burden  communities across Canada's Arctic  everywhere there's the suicide problem  what we experienced in the past the  resident just cool  relocation the loss of culture and  language has really affected a  separation from family when I went to  residential school I was nine and from  there on I only saw my parents like two  months of the year new to boots premier  pledge this week to tackle an epidemic  of suicide  the risks are particularly high in Inuit  communities unities range from five to  25 times the national rate Romani no  nevel national level knew enough to  accept allah meaning but while we hear  about the issues we don't often hear  much about what happens after the  headlines and how people in many of  these Arctic communities are working to  make things right  good morning Nana see ya a goal Naomi  memo Elizabeth the MINIX AGID project  began in 1989 after the tsunami of  disclosures made by victims of Eric the  Jaeger a convicted pedophile who wreaked  havoc across northern Canada where he  worked as a priest in the 1970s and 80s  I got really angry actually when these  children were abused sexually abuse in  the community I decided to try and do  something about it  those therapists were brought in from  southern Canada at the time it was clear  that in the long term it wouldn't be  enough when you bring our therapist in  to a small community they don't stay so  I bring their own way of counseling or  we call it maybe a blue knob ways a lot  of programs get started up by people  that come in and then sometimes they die  out as people leave and it loses  interest the fact that we've been in  operation for so long and will continue  to be here is you know it's a staple in  the community people always know that  there's some place to go to  Nanuk sujeet gets the majority of its  funding from the federal and territorial  governments and is run by a volunteer  board  International now the project does  everything from counseling and community  education to traditional skills courses  and trips out on the land what we  honestly it has done is combined in the  traditional knowledge and southern way  of counseling together I think it's  important that the community take  ownership of their own healing they also  offer services in English and Inuktitut  when a client wants to talk one-on-one  and this is where we are  patty manic is many exegetes community  workers they open up more about  especially when they're speaking  Inuktitut they start showing their  emotions their anger a baker lake woman  who has to be called Doris so the man  exegete project health savor life drop  tonight that's impossible from any type  of menifee like you in fact how do I  describe what a prototype  [Music]  semion making walks is the Nunavut MLA  for the Baker Lake riding he says he's  heard countless stories like Doris's  since Munich the chief began managing  project I buying is very unique in  journalists I guess the model that they  have to provide counseling services was  created was in the community so it's  more reflective of the needs that are  required I live with the people and I've  seen people from being heartbroken and  whatnot and where they're living a  positive life now today  so it really does make a change  [Music]  Joan cash flow in the past or at the  glad-tidings  Church in Baker Lake she's also a  community probation officer  she says she refers 70 to 80 percent of  her clients to the MINIX AGID project  and has seen the good it's done  firsthand it's very challenging to have  permanent counselors in small  communities especially in remote  communities like Baker Lake the mental  health workers don't stay very long  clients do not wish to constantly repeat  themselves to new workers we're resting  men to youth they start from square one  when their probation expires some of  them have actually told me they're going  to keep going there even if they don't  have to go there they know that the  doors are always open cashless says she  like people outside of Baker Lake to  look at the good the project does and to  help it expand there are so many issues  so many problems not only in the  community but across Nunavut there is so  much help needed in our community why is  the journal treatment center in the  region why is there no treatment center  in Nunavut with assistance from the  federal government not just the Nunavut  government but from the federal  government as well I really believe that  Manistee could expand their services to  work towards becoming a treatment center  something that the Inuit has been crying  for for a number of years Jude Law says  the challenges are still many but that  community run program incorporating  traditional culture and values into  mental health services is the first step  to healing not only for Baker Lake  but for Inuit communities across Canada  I had a father who was lost for 28 days  on the line at age 81 and he survived so  I tell young people  when they're talking about suicide  look at my father who did everything to  survive so I think it makes something  you know it's a lot to do with your  identity that you had back then and the  self-sufficiency of surviving on the  land I always say there's hope we will  survive  [Music]

